 ## Config Folder / Files and Bookmarks
 
-By default, the plugin doesn't sync config folder / files of obsidian (normally `.obsidian` folder) because it's one of the hidden files. 
+Enable **Settings → Remotely Save → Advanced settings → Sync complete usage environment** to sync themes, CSS snippets, appearance (including font size and colors), hotkeys, editor/core plugin settings, bookmarks, installed community plugin programs/resources/settings, and the enabled-plugin list. This reuses the existing `syncConfigDir` option. Changes transfer on the next manual or scheduled sync. Path filters and size limits still apply; include the configuration directory in any allow list, and allow files large enough for plugin programs.
 
-However, in the latest version, you can enable syncing config folder in settings. Please be aware that it's an experimental feature. Notably some config files' modified times are changed everytime when Obsidian is opened, and this destroys the assumptions of the plugin, so some information may not be synced properly.
+### Moving to a new device
 
-Moreover, the bookmarks of Obsidian are actually stored in `.obsidian/bookmarks.json` file. You can also set syncing this file (instead of the whole config folder) in settings. The plugin will keep the latest modified one across devices.
+1. On the original device, install this version, enable complete usage environment sync, and finish a successful sync.
+2. On the new device, install this version of Remotely Save and connect to the same remote vault/directory (and encryption password/method). Use the existing settings import/QR features or configure the connection manually. The default remote directory depends on the vault name, so use the same vault name or explicitly set the same remote directory.
+3. **Before the first normal sync**, choose **Restore usage environment from cloud** in advanced settings or the command palette. The source config folder normally is `.obsidian`; it can be mapped into the new device's current config folder for this restore.
+4. Restore uses cloud settings regardless of newer local default timestamps. It downloads and checks all files, backs up matching local files, then applies them. Missing plugin programs prevent applying the restore. Extra local files are retained, including extra files in existing plugins.
+5. Restart Obsidian immediately (fully quit and reopen on iOS). Restore enables configuration sync; further syncs pause until restart to keep in-memory old settings from being uploaded. Normal syncs that download environment changes also request a restart and pause further syncs.
+6. Run a normal sync after restart to download notes and attachments. Use the same configuration folder name on each device for ongoing environment syncing; restoring between different names is a one-time mapping.
+
+### Backups and limits
+
+Restore stores replaced files in `<configDir>/plugins/remotely-save/environment-backups/<restore-id>/original/`. `journal.json` records original paths, timestamps and whether each destination previously existed; `staged/` holds downloaded files. Failed writes trigger rollback. If rollback fails, syncing pauses and the backup location is shown. For manual recovery, close Obsidian, copy `original/` back into the config directory, remove newly created files recorded in `journal.json`, and reopen the app.
+
+Remotely Save itself (program, connection credentials and local backups) stays on each device and is ignored even if older versions uploaded it. Other plugins' settings may contain credentials; enable end-to-end encryption if those need to remain private in cloud storage. Device layouts (`workspace.json` and `workspace-mobile.json`), version-control folders and `node_modules` are excluded. System fonts, permissions, application-wide settings outside the vault and externally stored plugin data are outside this feature's scope. Desktop-only plugins still cannot run on mobile.
+
+For bookmarks only, leave full environment sync disabled and enable bookmark sync separately.
 
